{
 "id": "suspension",
 "name": "Suspension",
 "summary": "Gen 6 Camaro uses a double-pivot (dual lower ball joint) MacPherson strut front with aluminum lateral LCA and forward radius rod, and a five-link independent rear with stamped steel links, the coil spring and shock both on the rear lower control arm. ZL1 runs Magnetic Ride Control; ZL1 1LE runs Multimatic DSSV dampers with adjustable front ride height, rotatable camber top mounts and a 3-way rear bar. Race builds solid-mount the cradles, replace rubber bushings with spherical bearings, add camber/caster plates and adjustable links, and fit adjustable coilovers.",
 "overview": "Front: each front corner has a MacPherson strut clamped to the knuckle (2 bolts, 150 N·m + 80-95 deg) and bolted to the tower (3 bolts, 22 N·m). The lower knuckle is located by two separate aluminum links with their own ball joints - a lateral lower control arm and a forward-running radius rod (tension/caster rod) - both pivoting on the front cradle with torque-to-yield bolts (100 N·m + 90-105 deg, GM P/N 11611268 LCA, 11610915 radius rod). Steering is a ZF rack-mounted electric rack on the front cradle. A direct-acting sway bar on the front of the cradle links to the strut bodies.\n\nRear: five links per side - lower control arm (spring link: carries the spring and the shock; M14 camber eccentric inside), upper control arm, upper and lower trailing arms, and a toe link (GM 'adjust link', M12 toe eccentric inside) - all pivot on a steel rear cradle that mounts to the body through four bushings with TTY bolts (100 N·m + 90-105 deg) and is braced front and rear. Almost every rear pivot fastener is torque + angle and single-use per GM (BMR publishes the GM fastener P/N list).\n\nRace approach: (1) solid or Delrin cradle mounts (BMR BK062/BK063) and seam-welded cradle; (2) spherical bearings in all link bushings or rod-end links (BMR, SPL Parts); (3) camber/caster plates (BMR WAK360/361) and adjustable radius rods/LCAs (SPL TR CAM6, SPL FLCA CAM6, BMR FLC363) for -3 deg camber and equal caster; (4) adjustable rear links/lockouts (SPL EL CAM6, BMR TR007) so eccentrics cannot slip; (5) adjustable coilovers or retain DSSV; (6) adjustable bars and end links; (7) bump-steer tie rod ends (SPL TRE CAM6) when lowered.\n\nTypical race alignment starting points: front camber -2.7 to -3.5 deg, caster 7.5-9 deg (equal L/R), front toe 0 to 1/16 in out; rear camber -1.5 to -2.2 deg, rear toe 1/16-1/8 in total in. Typical spring rates (estimate): front coilover 600-1000 lb/in; rear depends on layout (factory pocket spring has a low motion ratio). Rules: NASA/SCCA classes restrict suspension changes by class (e.g. ST/TT/GT classes use points/power-to-weight; SCCA T1/T2 and NASA AmEx/ST classes differ) - check class rules before buying parts.\n\nCommon failure points: OE rear eccentrics slipping (toe/camber change), worn radius rod bushings (braking instability), cracked strut tower seams, bent aluminum front links after curbing, and bushings torqued at full droop.",
 "buildNotes": [
  "Rebuild/upgrade bushings and press bearings into links and knuckles on the bench before installation.",
  "Assemble front cradle with rack, sway bar, LCAs and radius rods; install cradle (with engine if doing a K-member drop-in) using new TTY bolts.",
  "Assemble struts (spring compressor), install struts and knuckles; leave all rubber-bushed TTY pivots loose.",
  "Assemble rear cradle with differential, five links, sway bar; raise and bolt in; install braces; then springs and shocks.",
  "Install hubs/brakes (phases 4) and wheels; set car on pads at ride height with ballast.",
  "Final-torque all TTY pivots at ride height with new bolts; paint-mark.",
  "Corner weight, then align (front camber/caster/toe, rear camber/toe); lock eccentrics; set sway bar links for zero preload.",
  "Shakedown: re-torque top mounts, check witness marks, zip-tie damper shafts for travel."
 ],
